58
00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:52.550
Justin Florentine: The silent one-shot paper.

59
00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:09.350
Gottfried Herold: I mean, Benedict recently had some idea of maybe one which might work, but at least even something conceptually that is EVQ is a cure, even something that is sort of one-shot and

60
00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:12.789
Gottfried Herold: Silent with silent setup, and…

61
00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:32.350
Gottfried Herold: NPQ, even something inefficient is sort of… I mean, we don't even have something inefficient, which is a silent setup. I mean, I might have some idea to get something, and PQ, it might have some idea to get something there, but still wouldn't solve the problem. There's just too much to be done in sort of… and it's just not there yet, and yeah.

62
00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.060
Gottfried Herold: Probably won't be for years.

63
00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:40.970
Justin Florentine: Okay, good enough. So, that brings us back to Lucid, our friend. And Lucid is…

64
00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:52.150
Justin Florentine: post-quantum secure as it stands right now, but unfortunately it has the optionality problem, and so that's what we're going to focus on discussing today.

65
00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:54.990
Justin Florentine: Let me just…

66
00:17:55.440 --> 00:18:08.859
Justin Florentine: pull this up here. So, maybe we can start with kind of our current ideas about preventing optionality, with a review of the notion of the key publisher.

67
00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:19.649
Justin Florentine: being kind of a role that's… I don't want to say enshrined in the protocol, but it is… or it's proposed to be…

68
00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:23.469
Justin Florentine: A somewhat trusted party.

69
00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:33.009
Justin Florentine: That would be neutrally responsible for revealing a key once an encryption transaction is committed to.

70
00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:51.319
Justin Florentine: Just as a reminder, this is… the optionality problem is exploitable because, you know, you can encrypt something, wait for it to appear on-chain, observe that, you know, your, bet, as it were, didn't go the way that you wanted to, and then withhold the key.

71
00:18:51.980 --> 00:19:07.269
Justin Florentine: So, tasking another party, with, with the key reveal after the commitment, is, is an approach. The question is, like, well, how do we, how do we track that they're neutral, etc. Oh, Andrew's joined us, cool.

72
00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:16.340
Justin Florentine: So… Jannik. This is, this is right now,

73
00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:22.429
Justin Florentine: An idea that we've kind of talked about maybe a month ago, and

74
00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:27.069
Justin Florentine: The next step was, great, how do we…

75
00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:41.750
Justin Florentine: track and trust that neutrality, right? And you posted a paper, which I linked to in our agenda, capturing some thoughts on a potential approach for that.

76
00:19:42.510 --> 00:20:02.020
Justin Florentine: And so thank you for that. I think that's… that's really great work. I know, Gottfried, you posted a whole bunch of, responses to it in Telegram. I also took a bunch of notes on it, and so let's… let's kick that discussion off. Where do we want to start?

77
00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:12.029
Jannik Luhn: If it's helpful, I could give a quick overview over the document, in case not everyone has read it.

78
00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:14.350
Justin Florentine: Let's do that.

79
00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:15.020
Jannik Luhn: Alright.

80
00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.149
Jannik Luhn: I can also share my screen, maybe.

81
00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:33.950
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, so… I mean, the basic, proposal, is already old,

82
00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:39.489
Jannik Luhn: The idea is to let the Validator set whitelist, or allow list,

83
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:44.450
Jannik Luhn: Some key publishers that they, deem trustworthy.

84
00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:51.840
Jannik Luhn: And then the question is how to concretely implement this, a long listing process. We need some sort of voting mechanism.

85
00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:57.970
Jannik Luhn: And I initially treated it as a black box, which was…

86
00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:14.770
Jannik Luhn: yeah, maybe good in the beginning, but not good enough, because there can be, like, details there that are problematic. So I came up with one proposal, I'm not sure if it's the best, or if there can be other proposals, but maybe something to get the discussion going.

87
00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:33.530
Jannik Luhn: Basically, what we need is a protocol where the validators can vote on addresses individually, so each address will be voted on individually, and when it receives 50% support of the validator set.

88
00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:39.389
Jannik Luhn: For example, in total effective balance, then it should get accepted by the protocol, basically.

89
00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:41.979
Jannik Luhn: There are some softer…

90
00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:52.729
Justin Florentine: So let's elaborate on what we mean by an address, right? This would be a signing address, right? So this would be an entity who we trust to have revealed the key, and their signature checks out, yeah?

91
00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:56.660
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, that would be identity of the key publisher, basically.

92
00:21:56.710 --> 00:21:57.580
Justin Florentine: Great.

93
00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:03.619
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, and requirements, software requirements, it should be efficient, of course.

94
00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:14.899
Jannik Luhn: I think there's potential to make these protocols very inefficient, because there's lots of Validators, and we need to count their votes very frequently, so I think that's, an important

95
00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:23.149
Jannik Luhn: configuration. We also want to support delegation, because most validators, especially, like, homesteaders, they probably don't want to

96
00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:32.059
Jannik Luhn: Make this, choice, and they also have to update it when they notice that some… a key publisher is behaving, like.

97
00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:36.900
Jannik Luhn: showing some fishy behavior, so, I think delegation is important.

98
00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.750
Justin Florentine: So, can we just, we just…

99
00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:44.949
Justin Florentine: elaborate on that a little bit, right? So, like, the assumption is that I need to delegate out my,

100
00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:57.700
Justin Florentine: audit and curation of this list, right? Like, so, like, I'm not gonna trust myself or take the time myself to actually track all of the, you know, keys that were delayed or never, ever released in order to maintain my own list.

101
00:22:57.700 --> 00:23:07.510
Justin Florentine: So I may want to delegate that out to somebody else that does a good job of holding people that fail to release their keys accountable.

102
00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:18.019
Jannik Luhn: Exactly. I mean, you might want to do it yourself if you're, like, a radical Homestaker, but in practice, you probably, or many probably want to delegate it to someone who is

103
00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:26.079
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, better knowledge, who observes what they do, who maybe has, yeah.

104
00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:31.310
Jannik Luhn: a special, a special, View of the network,

105
00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:36.650
Jannik Luhn: And as you said, maybe even, like, slashing contracts or something like that in place,

106
00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:41.480
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, it was just better position to make these, these decisions.

107
00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:54.630
Jannik Luhn: And responsiveness, ideally, should be very quick if, if the preferences of the value dataset changes, who…

108
00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:57.309
Jannik Luhn: Is a good key publisher and who isn't.

109
00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:05.599
Jannik Luhn: This preference should, be translated very quickly into, yeah, the set that the protocol then chooses.

110
00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:10.859
Jannik Luhn: And of course, since it's a voting protocol, it should be representative.

111
00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:16.049
Jannik Luhn: in the proposal that I have, it's the full value data set, so it's, by definition, I guess, representative.

112
00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:20.819
Jannik Luhn: You could imagine, that you also, choose a subset,

113
00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.809
Jannik Luhn: Which might also be fine.

114
00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:40.849
Jannik Luhn: Yes, and for the concrete implementation, I chose to do the bulk in a voting contract, so on the execution layer instead of the Consensus layer. I guess you could also do it in the Consensus layer. The reason why I chose a contract is because it's

115
00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:44.779
Jannik Luhn: You don't have to worry so much about,

116
00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.930
Jannik Luhn: Spam, because you have transaction fees, in place already.

117
00:24:50.530 --> 00:24:58.429
Jannik Luhn: I guess it's also easier to specify, because you have the language of solidity, you don't need to define, like.

118
00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.790
Jannik Luhn: Networking and stuff and so on.

119
00:25:01.210 --> 00:25:07.299
Jannik Luhn: yeah, I had another reason that I can't think about right now.

120
00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:16.639
Jannik Luhn: Mmm… and what this… contract… oh yeah, the other reason is that it's, kind of independent of,

121
00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:26.250
Jannik Luhn: So I thought one thought I had, that we could attach these messages, these voting messages to, for example.

122
00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:45.599
Jannik Luhn: block proposals or to attestations, but then that clashes a little bit with the responsiveness, because then you can only voice your opinion when you have to propose a block, for example, which takes time. And in a working contract, that's, like, completely separate. It does have downsides, though.

123
00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:51.220
Jannik Luhn: Because now a Validator needs to have, for example, to send, send transactions.

124
00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:53.880
Jannik Luhn: But, yeah, I guess it's a trade-off.

125
00:25:55.030 --> 00:26:06.169
Jannik Luhn: So what this contract does, it does, two things, mostly. One is it manages, ballot lists, like, lists of these addresses that gets accepted.

126
00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:13.230
Jannik Luhn: And that's a permissionless thing, so anyone can propose these lists and edit them, and…

127
00:26:13.390 --> 00:26:20.700
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, transfer ownership of them, and that's basically the delegates. The delegates who manage these lists.

128
00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:38.010
Jannik Luhn: And it… the other, functionality is that it provides a function where Validators can publish, their statement that they now are subscribed to a certain, list, and they follow this list. Even if it gets edited, they, follow it. So, yeah.

129
00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:46.840
Justin Florentine: We're using terms like ballot and list, kind of interchangeably, and I want to make sure that I'm understanding them correctly.

130
00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:48.310
Justin Florentine: the list.

131
00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:48.920
Jannik Luhn: Yes.

132
00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:54.319
Justin Florentine: is a list of key publishers, yeah? See, I'm thinking of that as a ballot.

133
00:26:54.430 --> 00:27:01.330
Justin Florentine: A ballot is, like, I vote for these people to be credibly neutral to release their keys. Is that correct?

134
00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:07.129
Jannik Luhn: Yes, that's correct. It's basically the same thing, I guess. I'm not sure if… Okay.

135
00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:08.410
Jannik Luhn: But distinguish, yeah.

136
00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:11.229
Justin Florentine: Yeah, it's just a writing thing, we can tighten that up. I'm a big…

137
00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:11.620
Jannik Luhn: Talk soon.

138
00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:22.719
Justin Florentine: model guy, I'm big on using a word that's kind of well-defined and resonates with people. Unfortunately, I am a native English speaker, too, so I have a lot of biases there.

139
00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:33.329
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, no, it's good to have these biases, especially from native English speakers. I guess ballot I chose because it came from the, kind of, the voting, aspect. List is the implementation.

140
00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:42.400
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, it is more concrete. It's also unordered, so it's not, technically, like, a linked list or anything, it's just, I guess, more of a map.

141
00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:46.120
Jannik Luhn: But that's an implementation detail.

142
00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:57.060
Jannik Luhn: Yes, I'm not sure if it… I mean, I only specified the interface, I guess the rest can be life-coded,

143
00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:07.030
Jannik Luhn: Theoretically, I haven't implemented this yet. We could, in order to prevent spam, require

144
00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:13.169
Jannik Luhn: key publishers or addresses, basically, to deposit some ETH before they can be added to a list.

145
00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:17.020
Jannik Luhn: And because the number of, addresses that exist,

146
00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:22.000
Jannik Luhn: yeah, reduces the efficiency of the protocol, I guess.

147
00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:28.890
Jannik Luhn: And, yeah, these are the… this is the SSV format now, how a, validator would

148
00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:33.730
Jannik Luhn: And also the follower list, so… Yeah, very simple message.

149
00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:40.199
Jannik Luhn: And then the second part of the proposal is the tallying, so how to actually, calculate the,

150
00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:58.289
Jannik Luhn: yeah, the outcome, which, based on these events that are emitted by the contract, how do we get to the list that we accept? And the important thing that I had in mind there when I came up with this is to make it incremental, so that we don't have to, at every block, recalculate everything.

151
00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:06.750
Jannik Luhn: But we only, start with kind of a zero initialization state, and then we process, the events one by one.

152
00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:11.489
Jannik Luhn: And… yeah, this updates our, our list.

153
00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:25.870
Jannik Luhn: like, the eventually accepted list. And that's, I think, works very well, efficiently. The only thing where it becomes, yeah, inefficient, I guess, is when we have to refresh the…

154
00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.780
Jannik Luhn: voting waits. So basically when the,

155
00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:33.839
Jannik Luhn: Effective balances of the Validators change, of course, when we have to update everything, basically.

156
00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:38.590
Jannik Luhn: But what we get in the end, I think that's…

157
00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:41.600
Jannik Luhn: basically unavoidable, I don't see a way around that.

158
00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:45.879
Jannik Luhn: But what we want to get in the end is,

159
00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:50.089
Jannik Luhn: Basically, yeah, the totally effective balance of,

160
00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:57.390
Jannik Luhn: That has voted for each individual address, and then we select those that have more than 50% of the effective balance.

161
00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:02.369
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, that's, like I said, the brief overview, I'm hoping.

162
00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:02.840
Gottfried Herold: I mean…

163
00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:04.830
Jannik Luhn: I'm hoping that was somewhat understandable.

164
00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:11.210
Gottfried Herold: Interesting. I mean, in theory, you could avoid it by subsampling a random subset each… after each…

165
00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:25.950
Gottfried Herold: After each, basically, epoch or whatever, just to only tally from a random subset or something. Because if you want to have a 50% anyway, I mean, typically, it might be okay if you just do, basically, a sub-selection.

166
00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:26.730
Justin Florentine: So…

167
00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:27.490
Gottfried Herold: with that.

168
00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:34.470
Justin Florentine: Let me, let me push back on that, and you can tell me, remind me that I'm not a cryptographer.

169
00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:39.959
Justin Florentine: Why should we use the effective balance at all?

170
00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:45.690
Justin Florentine: Does… does somebody who has more of a stake in the network…

171
00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:56.550
Justin Florentine: have more interest in ensuring that keys are revealed correctly. I don't see an obvious correlation there, do you?

172
00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:13.909
Gottfried Herold: No, I mean, as a cryptographer, if you ask me as a cryptographer, no, but as a mechanism design, the problem is, if you don't do that, then, consolidating balances becomes, basically reduces your voting weight, and this is something we do not want to incentivize.

173
00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:20.329
Justin Florentine: I see. Anders is making a similar point in chat.

174
00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:24.109
Justin Florentine: Can be gamed by splitting up stake, yeah.

175
00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:24.620
Gottfried Herold: I'm safe.

176
00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:31.269
Justin Florentine: Yeah, we disincentivize, yes, we disincentivize consolidation. Okay, good point. Great.

177
00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:52.610
Jannik Luhn: For sampling, I think that's interesting. I guess you have to… I don't know how big the effective balances differences are, if there can be a very big validator that has, I don't know, 30% of stake, like Lido, if they have every stake, everything in one address, you want to make sure that you count it.

178
00:31:52.870 --> 00:32:02.300
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I mean, you could instead just sample, instead of waiting, you could basically sample. Instead of basically waiting, you could basically just sample by weight, which is something we do anyway.

179
00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:07.039
Gottfried Herold: Yeah. And then… and then weigh normally.

180
00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:13.349
Jannik Luhn: What you could also do is you could count, like, this is just counting them unorderedly, you could,

181
00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:26.649
Jannik Luhn: start with the biggest validators, and then stop once you've reached 50%, and you don't have to count the rest. If one has zero votes after counting 50%, then you know they can't ever get over 50% again.

182
00:32:27.210 --> 00:32:31.299
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, me that… point is, I think this…

183
00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:43.369
Gottfried Herold: might not help you too much, because you would… you would go over all the validators, update, basically, your… your IDs, and then after that is done, you go over the IDs.

184
00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:45.970
Gottfried Herold: It's just much more efficient to do it that way.

185
00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:48.979
Jannik Luhn: I see, yeah.

186
00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:54.589
Gottfried Herold: So this doesn't… this optimization doesn't really give you that much, unfortunately.

187
00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:06.100
Jannik Luhn: I guess in the end, it's an implementation detail, and what would be interesting is to have a benchmark of that. I don't know how…

188
00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:09.700
Jannik Luhn: How fast this, currently is, how…

189
00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:11.820
Jannik Luhn: How fast the motion mechanism would be.

190
00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:30.259
Gottfried Herold: I mean, basically, it's… I mean, you have to update something, you have to update something that is sort of in memory, you have to do basically the same course as updating all the Validator balances once, just to update the weights for each of these lists once, and then you go through over all the lists.

191
00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:34.510
Jannik Luhn: Yep.

192
00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:41.360
Gottfried Herold: I mean, I expect that there is not too many lists, so the first part is sort of the… should be dominating.

193
00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:49.630
Justin Florentine: Okay. So, I guess, with regard to list count, and I think…

194
00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:58.049
Justin Florentine: Gottfried, you kind of alluded to this in some asynchronous comments. The costs of voting, right? Like, how…

195
00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:10.140
Justin Florentine: does it cost something to vote? Should it cost something to vote? There's an open question of, do we want to do this across the entire Validator population, or a subset?

196
00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:25.739
Justin Florentine: Is this a majority? And we also have 50% in there, right? Is that the right number? Is there maybe a plurality, that… that is required? Is it a supermajority that's required? You know, there's… there's…

197
00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:33.430
Justin Florentine: 50% makes intuitive sense, but I want to kind of explore maybe if there's other reasons to do that.

198
00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:40.229
Justin Florentine: Yeah, so I guess those are the three questions I have. I mean, let's just start with cost to voting, like.

199
00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:45.710
Justin Florentine: What are the costs That are kind of intrinsic to doing the voting.

200
00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:50.479
Justin Florentine: Do we need to reward people for paying those costs?

201
00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:55.700
Justin Florentine: Or do we treat this as, this is just a duty of the network, like, you have to, you have to do this?

202
00:34:58.570 --> 00:35:12.150
Gottfried Herold: I mean, the way that design currently is done, it's essentially… it's a contract, it's a transaction, so you have to pay for, basically, for a block space, that is there, for the, for,

203
00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:21.029
Gottfried Herold: The problem I see is that if you don't make anyone pay for it, you basically create, basically a free block space.

204
00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:35.450
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I think it goes in both directions. If you make it free, you get free block space, and you could spam the chain, potentially. If you…

205
00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:49.489
Jannik Luhn: You could also argue you want to pay them for doing so, because it's work that they have to do, but then it's, like, unclear how much you should pay them for. It's also unclear how many, like, the protocol doesn't know

206
00:35:50.160 --> 00:35:54.969
Jannik Luhn: How often a validator should vote, because it depends on the behavior of the key publishers.

207
00:35:55.990 --> 00:36:00.159
Jannik Luhn: So, I think that's very, very difficult to,

208
00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:06.260
Jannik Luhn: yeah, to come up with a good mechanism there, and I think just… Yeah.

209
00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:14.369
Jannik Luhn: I guess it's not a good answer for, for cryptoeconomics people, but I would just…

210
00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:19.130
Jannik Luhn: Keep it as is, and hope… People are honest.

211
00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:28.809
Gottfried Herold: I mean, of course, you could always record the blockade at which, sort of, your given Validator last voted to, sort of, limit and to rate limit things, so…

212
00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:32.840
Gottfried Herold: This kind of spam, that somebody abuses it for free block space.

213
00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:34.370
Gottfried Herold: What, yeah.

214
00:36:42.810 --> 00:37:01.840
Gottfried Herold: I mean, my expectation would be that, I mean, you could always say, hey, if… I mean, essentially, the entities who do the key reveal will be economically incentivized centralized entities, and they could just say, hey, I'm going to pay something for everybody who voted for a list which included me in this

215
00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:03.630
Gottfried Herold: In this timeframe or something.

216
00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:09.170
Gottfried Herold: Or with frame Transaction, they could even sponsor it.

217
00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:18.600
Jannik Luhn: And that could even be done trusted… trustlessly using… Exactly, exactly.

218
00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:20.570
Gottfried Herold: So, basically…

219
00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.780
Justin Florentine: Wait, could you elaborate on that? I'm not sure I followed the…

220
00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:30.730
Justin Florentine: I said, could you elaborate on that? I'm not quite sure that I followed that. Like, I get how you could use…

221
00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:34.300
Justin Florentine: Frame transactions to do it trustlessly.

222
00:37:37.220 --> 00:37:51.290
Gottfried Herold: What do you mean by trustlessly in the first place? I mean, you could, of course, make some contract that basically… that basically some Paymaster contract that pays for any such transaction that basically votes… includes a certain, key publisher address.

223
00:37:54.140 --> 00:37:54.770
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

224
00:37:54.770 --> 00:38:01.499
Jannik Luhn: I mean, you don't even need frame transactions for that, you can just have a bribery smart contract somewhere else that pays everyone who

225
00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:07.620
Jannik Luhn: voted for me, a certain amount of money, maybe even only if I get accepted in the end.

226
00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:24.959
Gottfried Herold: The problem is just to make sure that you don't vote for something else, or you don't vote a second time, or that this kind of.

227
00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:34.070
Justin Florentine: Yeah… ugh. On-chain voting is, like, playing with fire, man. This whole thing is… Spooky.

228
00:38:42.650 --> 00:38:49.890
Gottfried Herold: I mean, generally, I think it might be a good idea to sort of record the time when you last wrote it in the system contract.

229
00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:57.369
Gottfried Herold: If you want to sort of, incentivize such solutions, just to sort of help them

230
00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:01.259
Gottfried Herold: Prevent certain forms of abuse.

231
00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:09.140
Gottfried Herold: Because otherwise, they would have to record it.

232
00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:12.340
Gottfried Herold: And each of them would have to record it.

233
00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:13.980
Gottfried Herold: On their own.

234
00:39:40.970 --> 00:39:43.910
Justin Florentine: So, we're, we're… hmm…

235
00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:53.430
Justin Florentine: We are requiring decryption keys to be, you know, signed from the allow list, which means this entire mechanism is actually

236
00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:58.819
Justin Florentine: in protocol for its users, right? Like…

237
00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:04.880
Gottfried Herold: I mean, the CL needs to know it, it needs to get exported to the CL, and we basically just don't accept…

238
00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:11.450
Gottfried Herold: Elusive Transaction… If the, key population that is recorded there is not in the list.

239
00:40:12.580 --> 00:40:13.250
Justin Florentine: Yep.

240
00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:33.449
Justin Florentine: Anders, you're referring to the original shutter design that had the trust graph sort of thing in the front.

241
00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:36.800
Justin Florentine: of… on…

242
00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:44.140
Justin Florentine: Yeah, the trust graph that was kind of based on the ordering of the transactions on top of block, right? Yeah.

243
00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:55.550
Jannik Luhn: But that was not enough. Like, some people, criticized that you could still…

244
00:40:56.000 --> 00:41:00.209
Jannik Luhn: Like, even if… like, not… use the optionality, not the background.

245
00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:03.949
Jannik Luhn: Other transactions, but you just…

246
00:41:04.790 --> 00:41:08.849
Jannik Luhn: I don't know, some weird, arbitrage transactions.

247
00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:19.199
Justin Florentine: So it diluted the optionality, but didn't mitigate it.

248
00:41:21.460 --> 00:41:22.010
Jannik Luhn: Yep.

249
00:41:34.770 --> 00:41:36.499
Justin Florentine: It's not severe enough.

250
00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.230
Justin Florentine: Hell yeah, the builders don't like that.

251
00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.210
Justin Florentine: Hmm…

252
00:42:03.220 --> 00:42:06.689
Justin Florentine: Alright, sounds like this is complicated. Shocker.

253
00:42:06.880 --> 00:42:11.909
Justin Florentine: Do we have any other points that we want to cover? Do we want to continue to dwell on this?

254
00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:14.639
Justin Florentine: We're at about the halfway mark.

255
00:42:15.260 --> 00:42:16.390
Justin Florentine: Time-wise.

256
00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:29.470
Gottfried Herold: I mean, as I said, I mean, the 50% is, of course, something that is completely arbitrary, and basically any kind of threshold works. You could also make it…

257
00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:40.489
Gottfried Herold: more dynamic, if sort of the vote splits very, very strongly, that you might want to lower it dynamically. I guess 50% is just a shelling, a natural shelling point.

258
00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:44.940
Gottfried Herold: It's kind of a neutral, kind of unopinionated.

259
00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:52.680
Gottfried Herold: Sort of… It's basically an unopinionated neutral shelling point, which is good and nice.

260
00:42:52.820 --> 00:42:55.689
Gottfried Herold: But there is nothing in the design that enforces it.

261
00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:00.340
Justin Florentine: So, when we, when we talk about boats.

262
00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:12.069
Justin Florentine: Do we want to, you know, treat it kind of like, you know, the presence of a vote is an endorsement and the lack of one is an abstention? Or do we want to actually have upvotes and downvotes?

263
00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:18.919
Justin Florentine: Meaning, we should downscore this person because I think I caught them withholding a key.

264
00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.329
Justin Florentine: Or is that a distinction without a difference?

265
00:43:25.080 --> 00:43:30.659
Gottfried Herold: I mean, you can always vote for an empty list, basically, to explicitly abstain.

266
00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:32.150
Gottfried Herold: So…

267
00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:32.750
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

268
00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:35.100
Justin Florentine: Right.

269
00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:39.870
Jannik Luhn: I mean, you could argue maybe some validators have,

270
00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:48.890
Jannik Luhn: maybe I don't want to vote because I have no opinion, but maybe some validators have strong opinions that one key publisher is

271
00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:52.090
Jannik Luhn: Doing bad things, then that we might…

272
00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:54.900
Jannik Luhn: Give them, like, more negative thoughts, in a way.

273
00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:56.030
Jannik Luhn: Oh.

274
00:43:57.120 --> 00:44:01.650
Jannik Luhn: But, to be honest, I think I would keep it as simple as possible.

275
00:44:01.750 --> 00:44:04.219
Justin Florentine: Yeah, I'm kind of thinking that, too.

276
00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:16.900
Gottfried Herold: Yeah. I mean, the question is just whether you sort of make the tallies, or the 50%, basically among the total, weight of all… the total balance of all validators, or just among those validators that have voted.

277
00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:30.049
Gottfried Herold: Because if you make it among the total validators, then if participation into this mechanism is sort of low, then you basically get no key publisher at all, and that would be kind of a bad outcome.

278
00:44:30.050 --> 00:44:30.900
Justin Florentine: Wait…

279
00:44:34.170 --> 00:44:41.349
Justin Florentine: So, for instance, a regional outage, right? Where the attestation's, you know, weight goes down, or something like that.

280
00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:44.760
Justin Florentine: You lose Ability to reveal keys, right?

281
00:44:45.040 --> 00:44:56.519
Gottfried Herold: That is kind of… that's not a problem. The problem is, okay, so let's say we have some kind of fork where this feature goes live, but sort of… a lot of validators just don't bother with it, and they just don't do anything.

282
00:44:56.520 --> 00:45:07.799
Gottfried Herold: then, well, basically, we have zero registered key publishers, and nobody can send any Lucid messages, and we spend a lot of effort into enshrining a feature that is actually completely unusable.

283
00:45:09.800 --> 00:45:10.530
Justin Florentine: Yep.

284
00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:22.619
Jannik Luhn: We could also make the threshold dynamic in principle.

285
00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:28.930
Jannik Luhn: And let the Validator set vote on the threshold as well, similar to how they vote on gas limit.

286
00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:32.269
Jannik Luhn: Can they still do that? Let's still Work 2, at least.

287
00:45:33.710 --> 00:45:34.870
Gottfried Herold: That's a good point.

288
00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:52.610
Gottfried Herold: And just to answer Anders' question on the chat, I mean, since this is,

289
00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:56.950
Gottfried Herold: The point is… the point of this mechanism is that actually,

290
00:45:57.140 --> 00:46:11.229
Gottfried Herold: the attestation is basically a block, with a… which includes, or, a, a Lucid transaction which uses a key revealer that is not in the list is actually invalid.

291
00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:13.780
Gottfried Herold: That's basically the enforcement mechanism.

292
00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:25.409
Gottfried Herold: Because if you could use Lucid transactions with, sort of, key revealers outside of this mechanism, you run into the whole enshrine… the whole, problem again.

293
00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:35.979
Gottfried Herold: Or you mean you just use those, sort of, from the enshrined ones on top and below, sort of, the ones from others?

294
00:46:48.490 --> 00:46:52.250
Justin Florentine: Oh. Oh. Hmm, okay.

295
00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:56.830
Gottfried Herold: Hmm.

296
00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:07.230
Justin Florentine: So you order your top of block by… People that have… Key providers that have been…

297
00:47:07.900 --> 00:47:13.319
Justin Florentine: Vouched for. And if you don't, you're at the bottom of the top of block.

298
00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:18.290
Justin Florentine: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

299
00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:20.060
Justin Florentine: Huh.

300
00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:34.559
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, it does kind of protect against certain forms of bad MEV. I am not sure about, sort of, these kind of self-revealers who sort of want to, sort of.

301
00:47:35.030 --> 00:47:38.419
Gottfried Herold: Yeah. Decide not to reveal on their own.

302
00:47:38.650 --> 00:47:39.180
Justin Florentine: you're still…

303
00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:40.249
Gottfried Herold: You're able to read.

304
00:47:40.250 --> 00:47:47.989
Justin Florentine: Front-run the remainder of the block, just not the other people that are participating via a trusted key revealer.

305
00:47:48.180 --> 00:47:49.050
Gottfried Herold: Exactly.

306
00:47:54.980 --> 00:48:01.610
Justin Florentine: It… it is not as bad, and it does drive… it does drive… drive people to use it as well, right?

307
00:48:03.770 --> 00:48:07.539
Gottfried Herold: just feels like a bit… just feels a bit, I don't know, half-baked, you know?

308
00:48:10.780 --> 00:48:12.339
Justin Florentine: Feels like a half measure.

309
00:48:18.350 --> 00:48:25.259
Jannik Luhn: On the topic of the threshold, it should probably be higher than the biggest zkger we have right now.

310
00:48:26.270 --> 00:48:31.050
Jannik Luhn: Otherwise, one big centralized sticker could get in a key publisher.

311
00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:32.920
Jannik Luhn: Just purely on their own.

312
00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:35.060
Justin Florentine: Refined.

313
00:48:35.790 --> 00:48:37.070
Gottfried Herold: That's a good point.

314
00:48:39.070 --> 00:48:40.130
Justin Florentine: Sure.

315
00:48:49.470 --> 00:48:53.809
Gottfried Herold: Maybe you should also allow negative votes and count those double or whatever.

316
00:48:57.240 --> 00:49:00.049
Justin Florentine: Yeah, negative voting introduces more motive.

317
00:49:02.220 --> 00:49:08.260
Justin Florentine: I'm voting… upvoting this person because they have a track record of being trusted, and I'm downvoting this person because…

318
00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:11.560
Justin Florentine: their stake is too big. Those are, those are two different…

319
00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:15.530
Justin Florentine: Intent… intents that are being captured.

320
00:49:22.350 --> 00:49:24.500
Justin Florentine: Still don't think it's worth the complexity.

321
00:49:27.870 --> 00:49:28.510
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

322
00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:32.870
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I guess 50% of all stakes are kind of a reasonable shelling point.

323
00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:35.330
Justin Florentine: Yeah, I think that's a good starting point.

324
00:49:40.610 --> 00:49:44.150
Justin Florentine: Okay, I'm gonna capture this idea about…

325
00:49:50.780 --> 00:49:52.419
Justin Florentine: Top of block ordering.

326
00:49:52.790 --> 00:50:10.750
Justin Florentine: So, top of block ordering can be used to prioritize… The use of… Who allow listed providers.

327
00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:12.400
Justin Florentine: Over.

328
00:50:12.930 --> 00:50:14.690
Justin Florentine: Unknown.

329
00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:17.750
Justin Florentine: ones.

330
00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:34.609
Justin Florentine: Which also asserts that we don't… we no longer reject the… we don't actually check the list. Well, we check the list when we're prioritizing top of block.

331
00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:37.469
Justin Florentine: That's the only time you check it.

332
00:50:47.710 --> 00:50:49.880
Justin Florentine: Okay. Incremental improvement.

333
00:50:51.670 --> 00:51:00.000
Gottfried Herold: I think it would also sort of alleviate, sort of, these, market entry barrier issues that I think are basically the most…

334
00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:02.549
Gottfried Herold: Are my main concerns, actually, with this.

335
00:51:05.280 --> 00:51:06.690
Justin Florentine: How does it alleviate them?

336
00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:12.419
Gottfried Herold: Because you can still… I mean, okay, so let's say you are some kind of provider.

337
00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:12.750
Justin Florentine: Yep.

338
00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:29.839
Gottfried Herold: who does this, and who does a good… I mean, you are basically a financially incentivized entity who does basically do this kind of key reveal service, and of course, you collect fees for that, you have a lot of expensive infrastructure and everything, and now your approval rating among the…

339
00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:50.289
Gottfried Herold: Validators, basically is like, I don't know, 51%, mostly because Lido got bribed by your competitor, who wants you to go out of business so they can take over your share. Now, you basically go from 51% to 49%, and one side of a sudden, all your revenue goes down to zero, and you go bankrupt.

